NNAAMMEE
       rasttopnm - convert a Sun rasterfile to a PNM image


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       rraassttttooppnnmm

       [--iinnddeexx]

       [_r_a_s_t_f_i_l_e]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       rraassttttooppnnmm  reads  a Sun rasterfile as input and produces a PNM image as
       output.  The type of the output file depends on the  input  file  -  if
       it's black and white, rraassttttooppnnmm writes a PBM image.  If it's grayscale,
       rraassttttooppnnmm writes a PGM.  If it's color, rraassttttooppnnmm writes  a  PPM.   The
       program tells you which type it is generating.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), rraassttttooppnnmm recognizes  the
       following command line option:




       --iinnddeexx This  odd option tells rraassttttooppnnmm to use the color map indices in
              the raster as if they are the color values.

              In a colormapped Sun rasterfile, the header of the file contains
              a  color  map,  associating each unique color that occurs in the
              file with an index number.  The raster portion of the file  then
              represents  the  color of a pixel with one of those colormap in‐
              dices.

              This option has found use as follows: take a  regular  grayscale
              Sun rasterfile in which all the grayscale value are represented,
              so the colormap indices are identical to the color values.  Mod‐
              ify  its  color  map  so as to alter the colors in the image, in
              particular to equalize the colors.  With that  modified  raster‐
              file  as  input,  rraassttttooppnnmm without --iinnddeexx produces the modified
              image.  But with --iinnddeexx, it produces the original unmodified im‐
              age.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.56 (September 2011).




